// Title: Potential UAC Bypass Via Sdclt.EXE
// Sigma ID: 40f9af16-589d-4984-b78d-8c2aec023197
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Roberto Rodriguez (Cyb3rWard0g), OTR (Open Threat Research)
// Date: 2020-05-02 | Modified: 2024-12-01
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, T1548.002
// Description: A General detection for sdclt being spawned as an elevated process. This could be an indicator
//   of sdclt being used for bypass UAC techniques.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://github.com/OTRF/detection-hackathon-apt29/issues/6
//   - https://github.com/OTRF/ThreatHunter-Playbook/blob/2d4257f630f4c9770f78d0c1df059f891ffc3fec/docs/evals/apt29/detections/3.B.2_C36B49B5-DF58-4A34-9FE9-56189B9DEFEA.md
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_uac_bypass_sdclt.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "sdclt.exe" and (ProcessIntegrityLevel in~ ("High", "S-1-16-12288"))
